WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.833
Clauses and Complete Sentences

00:00:03.213 --> 00:00:06.154
Every sentence needs a subject and a main verb.

00:00:07.354 --> 00:00:08.444
How to spot it

00:00:08.844 --> 00:00:11.892
Same words, different punctuation or verb form

00:00:12.442 --> 00:00:16.027
The question says “conventions of Standard English”

00:00:16.577 --> 00:00:20.081
It’s testing: where one complete sentence ends

00:00:21.281 --> 00:00:22.048
The method

00:00:22.448 --> 00:00:24.917
Step 1: Find the subject

00:00:25.197 --> 00:00:27.162
Who or what is the sentence about?

00:00:27.712 --> 00:00:29.775
Step 2: Find its main verb

00:00:30.055 --> 00:00:32.940
Words like “stretching” or “to stretch” don’t count.

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:36.403
Step 3: Test each side of the blank

00:00:36.683 --> 00:00:38.857
Could it stand alone as a sentence?

00:00:39.407 --> 00:00:43.039
Step 4: Two full sentences need a real break

00:00:43.319 --> 00:00:46.506
A period, a semicolon, or comma + FANBOYS.

00:00:47.056 --> 00:00:48.151
In a few words

00:00:48.301 --> 00:00:51.582
Subject. Verb. Can it stand alone?

00:00:52.782 --> 00:00:53.563
The rules

00:00:53.963 --> 00:00:56.490
Subject + main verb = sentence

00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:58.191
Wrong: The storm ending.

00:00:58.471 --> 00:00:59.936
Right: The storm ended.

00:01:00.486 --> 00:01:02.722
-ing and “to” verbs aren’t main verbs

00:01:03.002 --> 00:01:05.839
Wrong: The reef stretching for miles.

00:01:06.119 --> 00:01:08.843
Right: The reef stretches for miles.

00:01:09.393 --> 00:01:11.894
Because, although, which make it incomplete

00:01:12.174 --> 00:01:13.566
Wrong: Because it rained.

00:01:13.846 --> 00:01:16.050
Right: Because it rained, we stayed in.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:19.363
A comma alone can’t join two sentences

00:01:19.643 --> 00:01:22.722
Wrong: The storm ended, we went outside.

00:01:23.002 --> 00:01:26.047
Right: The storm ended. We went outside.

00:01:26.597 --> 00:01:28.927
No punctuation at all is a run-on

00:01:29.207 --> 00:01:32.359
Wrong: The storm ended we went outside.

00:01:32.639 --> 00:01:35.438
Right: The storm ended; we went outside.

00:01:36.638 --> 00:01:37.699
Let’s try one

00:01:38.099 --> 00:01:44.191
The Atacama Desert in Chile, one of the driest places on Earth, ______ some weather stations there

00:01:44.191 --> 00:01:45.844
have never recorded rain.

00:01:46.494 --> 00:01:49.190
The subject is the Atacama Desert.

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:51.921
The part between the commas is extra.

00:01:51.921 --> 00:01:55.188
So where’s the main verb? There isn’t one yet.

00:01:55.738 --> 00:02:00.751
Receiving, having received, to receive: none of these can be a main verb.

00:02:01.301 --> 00:02:05.872
Receives is a real main verb, so the sentence is complete. Choice A.

00:02:07.072 --> 00:02:08.141
Common traps

00:02:08.541 --> 00:02:09.763
The long fragment

00:02:10.043 --> 00:02:13.101
Forty words with no main verb is still a fragment.

00:02:13.651 --> 00:02:14.873
The comma splice

00:02:15.153 --> 00:02:17.973
Two full sentences glued with only a comma.

00:02:19.173 --> 00:02:21.406
Clauses and Complete Sentences

00:02:21.686 --> 00:02:24.967
Subject. Verb. Can it stand alone?

00:02:25.517 --> 00:02:26.097
Spot it

00:02:26.297 --> 00:02:29.952
Same words; punctuation or verb form changes

00:02:30.502 --> 00:02:31.209
Steps

00:02:31.409 --> 00:02:32.920
1. Find the subject

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:36.949
2. Find its main verb (not -ing or to + verb)

00:02:37.229 --> 00:02:40.398
3. Test each side: can it stand alone?

00:02:40.678 --> 00:02:45.110
4. 2 sentences → period, semicolon, or comma + FANBOYS

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:46.385
Remember

00:02:46.585 --> 00:02:49.181
Sentence = subject + main verb

00:02:49.461 --> 00:02:51.910
-ing / to + verb ≠ main verb

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:54.856
because, although, which → can’t stand alone

00:02:55.136 --> 00:02:57.675
Comma alone can’t join 2 sentences

00:02:58.225 --> 00:02:58.666
Trap

00:02:58.866 --> 00:03:01.131
A long “sentence” with no main verb

00:03:01.881 --> 00:03:05.299
